"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][17]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M17-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M17-L1 – Estimation Theory, Sufficiency and Fisher Information<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Master: Mathematical Statistics and Statistical Learning (10 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L2<\/a>,\nM6.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Identify sufficient statistics\nand derive Fisher information and the Cramér–Rao bound.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What data does estimation require?<\/h2>\n<p>A measuring device records independent waiting times\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin minutes. The exponential density with unknown rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x;\\lambda)=\\lambda e^{-\\lambda x}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\geq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe rate has units of 1/min. A sample contains not only information\nabout the rate but also the ordering of measurements and random\ndifferences. Which of these are necessary for estimating the rate?<\/p>\n<p>For the joint density at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/munderover><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><munderover><mo>∏<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/munderover><msub><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L(\\lambda;x)=\\lambda^n\\exp\\left(-\\lambda\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\\right)\\prod_{i=1}^n\\mathbf{1}_{x_i\\geq0}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The factorisation criterion states for dominated models that if the\ndensity can be expressed as a parameter-dependent function of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntimes a parameter-free function of the data, then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis sufficient. Here the sum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=\\sum_i X_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsuffices. The conditional distribution of the sample given\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nno longer depends on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a statement within the model, not a general recommendation to\ndelete measurement series: with dependence or time-varying rates, the\nordering may be important.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Information as curvature<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001597_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Normierte Log-Likelihoods für n=5 und n=20 bei gleicher geschätzter Rate 0,5 pro Minute.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Normierte Log-Likelihoods für n=5 und\nn=20 bei gleicher geschätzter Rate 0,5 pro Minute.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Krümmung der Log-Likelihood.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The log-likelihood\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell=n\\log\\lambda-\\lambda T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas score\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=n/\\lambda-T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand second derivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-n/\\lambda^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder regularity conditions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[U]=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe expected information is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mi>U<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>n<\/mi><msup><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_n(\\lambda)=E[U^2]=-E[\\ell&#39;&#39;(\\lambda)]=\\frac{n}{\\lambda^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The equality follows by differentiating the normalisation of the\ndensity under the integral. Independent observations contribute additive\ninformation. The observed information\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\ell&#39;&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nequals the expected information here, but not in general. The more\nsharply curved the log-likelihood is near its maximum, the more\nsensitive it is to departures from the parameter value; the graphic\nillustrates this for two samples with the same mean waiting time.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Efficiency and bias<\/h2>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand measurements\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1,2,1,3,3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmin. The score vanishes at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>λ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat\\lambda=n/T=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n1/min. The second derivative is negative, so this is a maximum. However,\nthe estimator is upwardly biased for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Gamma<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">Rate <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\sim\\operatorname{Gamma}(n,\\text{Rate }\\lambda)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[1/T]=\\lambda/(n-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(n-1)/T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis unbiased and equals 0.4 1/min here.<\/p>\n<p>For regular models and unbiased estimators\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>λ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widetilde\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>λ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(\\widetilde\\lambda)\\geq 1/I_n(\\lambda)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt the true rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis bound is 0.05\n(1/min)<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi><\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n&gt;2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe corrected estimator has variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda^2/(n-2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhere approximately 0.0833. The lower bound need not be attained.\nRao–Blackwellisation replaces an estimator with its conditional\nexpectation given a sufficient statistic; under squared loss this cannot\nincrease the risk. Completeness is an additional property and does not\nfollow from sufficiency.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Ten waiting times sum to 25 min. Determine the ML estimator, the\nunbiased estimator and the information at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat information would be lost if the plant had shift-dependent\nrates?<\/p>\n<p>Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>25<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10/25=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>25<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.36<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9/25=0.36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n1/min;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.16<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>62.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_{10}=10/0.16=62.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmin<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi><\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Cramér–Rao bound is 0.016\n(1/min)<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi><\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor two shift-specific rates, at least the corresponding totals and\ncounts are required, not merely the overall total. When the distribution\nis unknown, these efficiency statements cannot be transferred without\nchecking the model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.stat.berkeley.edu/~wfithian/courses/stat210a/sufficiency.html\">UC\nBerkeley, Will Fithian: Sufficiency<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Definition, factorisation<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.stat.umn.edu/geyer/5421/notes/likelihood.html\">University\nof Minnesota, Charles Geyer: Likelihood Inference<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Fisher\ninformation and likelihood ratio<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M17-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M17-L2 – Consistency, Asymptotic Normality and the Delta Method<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Master: Mathematical Statistics and Statistical Learning (10 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M16&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M16-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M4-L7<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish consistency from\nlimiting distributions and apply the delta method to a transformed\nestimate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A limit is not the same as an expectation<\/h2>\n<p>An estimator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis consistent for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif, for every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_\\theta(|T_n-\\theta|&gt;\\varepsilon)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntends to zero. Unbiasedness, by contrast, concerns a specific\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor example, the estimator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar X_n+1/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis consistent for the expectation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprovided the independent, identically distributed observations have a\nfinite first moment, even though it is systematically shifted by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBy contrast, the estimator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis unbiased but not consistent for a non-degenerate distribution.<\/p>\n<p>With finite positive variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe central limit theorem additionally gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mover><mo>→<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mover><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{n}(\\bar X_n-\\mu)\\xrightarrow{d}N(0,\\sigma^2).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The distribution of the scaled error therefore has a non-trivial\nlimit. The original estimate, by contrast, concentrates at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConvergence in probability and convergence in distribution are distinct\nstatements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The delta method from a Taylor expansion<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mover><mo>→<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mover><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt n(T_n-\\theta)\\xrightarrow{d}N(0,v)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe continuously differentiable near\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Taylor expansion gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>o<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(T_n)-g(\\theta)=g&#39;(\\theta)(T_n-\\theta)+o_P(|T_n-\\theta|)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConsequently,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mover><mo>→<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mover><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt n[g(T_n)-g(\\theta)]\\xrightarrow{d}N(0,[g&#39;(\\theta)]^2v).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The little\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>o<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">o_P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes a remainder that is negligible in probability. With several\nparameters the derivative is replaced by the Jacobian\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J_g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the limiting covariance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><msubsup><mi>J<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J_g\\Sigma J_g^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA zero derivative leads to a degenerate approximation; a higher-order\nexpansion may be necessary.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Case study: costs per geometric size factor<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001550_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Logarithmus und lokale Tangente bei x=4; die Eingangsabweichung wird durch die Steigung 1/4 skaliert.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Logarithmus und lokale Tangente bei x=4;\ndie Eingangsabweichung wird durch die Steigung 1/4\nskaliert.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Delta-Methode am Logarithmus.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>100 independent positive measurements have sample mean 4 and standard\ndeviation 2. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(\\mu)=\\log\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(measurements expressed as dimensionless ratios),\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g&#39;(\\mu)=1/\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe approximate standard error of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mover><mi>X<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log\\bar X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msqrt><mn>100<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/(4\\sqrt{100})=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn asymptotic 95% interval on the log scale is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>±<\/mo><mn>1.96<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log4\\pm1.96\\cdot0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTransforming back gives approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>3.627<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4.412<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[3.627,4.412]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the arithmetic expectation. It is not an interval for the\nexpectation of the log-transformed measurements. The graphic illustrates\nthe local tangent rather than a globally linear transformation.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>n, mean, sd <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">4.0<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">2.0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>se <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> sd <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> (mean <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> np.sqrt(n))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ci <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.exp(np.log(mean) <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]) <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1.96<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> se)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(ci, [<span class=\"fl\">3.62659<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">4.41185<\/span>], atol<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">0.0001<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(se, ci)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Transform\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(t)=1/t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen the true value is 5 and the asymptotic variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate the approximate standard error at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSolution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g&#39;(5)=-1/25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nlimiting variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>625<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0144<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9/625=0.0144<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand standard error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.012<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.012<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNear zero the transformation is ill-conditioned. Small samples, heavy\ntails, dependence and boundary parameters require separate arguments. A\nlimiting distribution alone does not guarantee a particular coverage\nrate at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://faculty.washington.edu/yenchic/26A_stat581/Lec2_large.html\">University\nof Washington: STAT 581, Introduction of large sample theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Delta\nmethod and limiting distributions<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M17-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M17-L3 – Likelihood Ratios and Asymptotic Tests<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Master: Mathematical Statistics and Statistical Learning (10 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L2<\/a>,\nM6.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare nested models using\nlikelihood-ratio, Wald and score tests.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two models, one dataset<\/h2>\n<p>A batch of 80 independently tested components contains 52 successful\ntests. The null hypothesis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>:<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0:p=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to a specified reference. The alternative allows every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\in(0,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth models use the same sample, Bernoulli distribution and data\nencoding. The alternative contains the null model: it can fit at least\nas well.<\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>52<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=52<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell(p)=k\\log p+(n-k)\\log(1-p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe alternative reaches its maximum at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.65<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat p=0.65<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile the constrained maximum is at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_0=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDefine<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Lambda=\\frac{L(p_0)}{L(\\widehat p)},\\qquad D=-2\\log\\Lambda=2[\\ell(\\widehat p)-\\ell(p_0)].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Wilks’ theorem and comparison tests<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001625_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Log-Likelihood relativ zum Maximum, eingeschränkter Wert p=0,5 und freier Wert p=0,65.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Log-Likelihood relativ zum Maximum,\neingeschränkter Wert p=0,5 und freier Wert p=0,65.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Likelihood-Ratio-Test.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Under the null hypothesis, for regular, identifiable nested models\nwith the true parameter in the interior and suitable differentiability,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconverges to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msubsup><mi>χ<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi^2_q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the difference in the dimensions of the free parameters. In the\nexample\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe log-likelihood difference is about 3.656;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>7.312<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D\\approx7.312<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe upper-tail probability of the chi-squared distribution is\napproximately 0.00685. The graphic shows the two evaluated points on the\nsame log-likelihood.<\/p>\n<p>The Wald test measures the departure from the null value in units of\nthe standard error evaluated at the unrestricted maximum:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W=n(\\widehat p-p_0)^2/[\\widehat p(1-\\widehat p)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe score test uses the slope and information under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=n(\\widehat p-p_0)^2/[p_0(1-p_0)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder the stated conditions the three statistics are asymptotically\nequivalent under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut are not exactly identical.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> log<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.stats <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> chi2<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>n, k, p0 <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">80<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">52<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> k <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> n<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ell <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> z: k<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>log(z) <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> (n<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>k)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>log(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>z)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>d <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(ell(p)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>ell(p0))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> n<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(p<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p0)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(p<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>q <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> n<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(p<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p0)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(p0<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p0))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(d, w, q, chi2.sf(d, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">7.30<\/span> <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> d <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">7.32<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(q<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">7.2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-10<\/span><\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of application<\/h2>\n<p>A test of a variance component equal to zero lies on the boundary of\nthe permissible parameter space. In a mixture model, components may be\nunidentifiable under the null hypothesis. In either situation the usual\nchi-squared calibration must not be applied blindly. Depending on the\nmodel, special limiting distributions or parametric simulations under\nthe null hypothesis are required. For small Bernoulli samples an exact\nbinomial test is an alternative. The test decision is not a probability\nthat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis true and says little about the practical importance of a departure.\nTherefore supplement it with the estimate and an interval.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>A model with four regression coefficients has a maximised\nlog-likelihood of -110. A nested model that sets two coefficients to\nzero reaches -114. All other parameters are parametrised identically.\nDetermine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe degrees of freedom and the asymptotic decision at 5%. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=2\\cdot4=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.0183<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=\\exp(-8/2)\\approx0.0183<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe restriction is rejected under the regularity assumptions; this does\nnot establish a causal effect.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.stat.umn.edu/geyer/5421/notes/likelihood.html\">University\nof Minnesota, Charles Geyer: Likelihood Inference<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Fisher\ninformation and likelihood ratio<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M17-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M17-L4 – Non-parametric Density Estimation and Smoothing<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Master: Mathematical Statistics and Statistical Learning (10 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4, M5, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate a kernel density\nestimate, justify the bandwidth and explain the bias–variance\ntrade-off.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Density instead of fixed bins<\/h2>\n<p>A sensor supplies calibrated deviations in millimetres. A histogram\ndepends on bin boundaries; kernel density estimation instead places a\nsmall density around each observation. For independent observations\ndrawn from an unknown density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a non-negative normalised kernel\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>f<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/munderover><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mi>h<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>h<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat f_h(x)=\\frac{1}{nh}\\sum_{i=1}^n K\\left(\\frac{x-X_i}{h}\\right),\\qquad h&gt;0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The bandwidth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas units of mm, and the density has units of 1/mm. After substitution\neach summand integrates to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso the total area is one. A Gaussian kernel\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K(u)=\\exp(-u^2/2)/\\sqrt{2\\pi}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas infinite support. A compact kernel, by contrast, contributes only\nwithin a limited neighbourhood. A density height may exceed one; only\nintegrated areas are probabilities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three bandwidths, the same observations<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001604_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Gauß-Kerndichten der fünf Messwerte bei Bandbreiten 0,15, 0,45 und 1,2 mm.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Gauß-Kerndichten der fünf Messwerte bei\nBandbreiten 0,15, 0,45 und 1,2 mm.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Drei Bandbreiten einer Kerndichte.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For the measurements\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-1.4,-1,-0.8,0.9,1.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe graphic shows densities with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.45<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.45<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmm. Small bandwidths isolate individual measurements; large ones may\nmerge two genuinely separate populations into a single peak. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.45<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h=0.45<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe two groups may remain visible. This is an exploratory observation,\nnot a significance test for multimodality.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.4<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">1.3<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>grid <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.linspace(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">20001<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> kde(q, h):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    u <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (np.asarray(q)[..., <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>h<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> np.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>u<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>u<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>).mean(axis<span class=\"op\">=-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(h<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> h <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [<span class=\"fl\">.15<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.45<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">1.2<\/span>]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    density <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> kde(grid, h)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(np.trapezoid(density, grid)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(h, kde(<span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>, h))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Smoothing and model selection<\/h2>\n<p>For a twice continuously differentiable density, interior points, a\nsymmetric kernel with finite second moment, and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h\\to0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">nh\\to\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe leading bias is of order\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the variance of order\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(nh)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder suitable integrability conditions the integrated mean squared\nerror is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_1h^4+C_2/(nh)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith positive constants. Differentiation with respect to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4C_1h^3-C_2/(nh^2)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence the optimal order\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^{-1/5}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the smooth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-dimensional\ncase with isotropic bandwidth, the variance order increases to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(nh^d)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe corresponding bandwidth order is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^{-1/(d+4)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis illustrates the curse of dimensionality.<\/p>\n<p>In practice, one can choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing leave-one-out log-likelihood rather than density at the same\ntraining points. With bounded support, such as exclusively positive\ntimes, a Gaussian kernel may assign mass outside the permissible region.\nBoundary corrections or suitable transformations should then be\nconsidered. Local kernel regression uses similar weights to estimate a\nconditional mean, but is not density estimation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>How does the asymptotically optimal one-dimensional bandwidth change\nwhen the sample size increases by a factor of 32 and the distribution\nremains unchanged? Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>32<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">32^{-1/5}=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe leading variance falls to one thirty-second, but the smoothing bias\nremains. Also explain why a bandwidth of 0.45 mm must be converted to\n0.00045 m when the data are expressed in metres.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://web.eecs.umich.edu/~cscott/past_courses/eecs598w14/notes/19_kde.pdf\">University\nof Michigan, Clayton Scott: Kernel Density Estimation (EECS 598)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nDefinition, kernel, bandwidth; PDF and first page opened visually<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M17-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M17-L5 – Statistical Learning Theory and Capacity Control<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Master: Mathematical Statistics and Statistical Learning (10 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M10, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Justify empirical risk\nminimisation and control a finite hypothesis class using a probability\nbound.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Risk and selection<\/h2>\n<p>A quality-control system can choose among several fixed decision\nrules. Each rule\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmaps features to a class. For a random observation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=(X,Y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand loss\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell(h,Z)\\in[0,1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe population risk is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(h)=E[\\ell(h,Z)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe expectation refers to new data from the target distribution. On\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent training observations, we estimate this risk by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_n(h)=n^{-1}\\sum_i\\ell(h,Z_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEmpirical risk minimisation (ERM) selects a minimiser\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>h<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the predetermined class\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℋ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>Even if the empirical estimate is unbiased for each fixed rule, the\nrule selected afterwards as best may appear unduly favourable. Therefore\na concentration statement for an individual rule is insufficient.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Deriving the simultaneous bound<\/h2>\n<p>For every fixed rule, Hoeffding’s inequality gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><msup><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(|R_n(h)-R(h)|&gt;\\varepsilon)\\leq2e^{-2n\\varepsilon^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a finite class of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrules the union bound limits the probability of at least one violation\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><msup><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2me^{-2n\\varepsilon^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSet this bound equal to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>δ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand solve for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon(n,m,\\delta)=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2m/\\delta)}{2n}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With probability at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover the training sample, the error bound holds simultaneously for every\nrule, including the one selected from the data. On this event, for the\ntrue risk minimiser\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithin the class it follows that<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>h<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>h<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R(\\widehat h)\\leq R_n(\\widehat h)+\\varepsilon\\leq R_n(h^*)+\\varepsilon\\leq R(h^*)+2\\varepsilon.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is an excess-risk bound; it does not include the gap between the\nclass and the globally optimal Bayes classifier.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Applying the bound to model capacity<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001565_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Hoeffding-Union-Schranke nach Anzahl fester Regeln bei n=1000 und n=4000, Delta 0,05.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Hoeffding-Union-Schranke nach Anzahl\nfester Regeln bei n=1000 und n=4000, Delta 0,05.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Kapazität und Fehlergrenze.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>64<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=64<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.06265<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon\\approx0.06265<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt a training error of 0.08, the upper risk bound is therefore\napproximately 0.143, provided the rules were fixed before the sample.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4096<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=4096<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe deviation bound rises to around 0.0775. The graphic shows this\nlogarithmic dependence. Bounds may be conservative; validation and\ntheoretical control answer different questions. For continuous model\nfamilies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not finite; the following lesson replaces simple counting with a\ncombinatorial measure of capacity. For unbounded squared losses,\nHoeffding’s inequality in this form is not applicable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>What is the minimum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequired so that, for 100 fixed rules and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe deviation bound is at most 0.05?<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> log, ceil, sqrt<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>n <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> ceil(log(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">100<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.05<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.05<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> n <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1659<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> sqrt(log(<span class=\"dv\">4000<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>n)) <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.05<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(n)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Solution: 1659 observations; the corresponding ERM excess-risk bound\nis 0.10 rather than 0.05. If arbitrary new rules were first invented\nusing a dataset, the number of rules remaining at the end cannot simply\nbe inserted into the formula.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~dsuth/532D/26w1/notes/html/chap_finite-classes.html\">UBC:\nStatistical Learning Theory, ERM with finite hypothesis classes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nFinite classes, Hoeffding and union bound<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://gradml.mit.edu/supervised/learnability_and_vc/\">MIT\n6.790: Learnability and VC Dimension<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Shattering, growth\nfunction<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M17-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M17-L6 – VC Dimension and Generalisation Bounds<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Master: Mathematical Statistics and Statistical Learning (10 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L5<\/a>,\nM2.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine shattering, VC\ndimension and statistical capacity using an infinite class of interval\nrules.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Effective number of classifications<\/h2>\n<p>An infinite set of models can generate only finitely many distinct\nbinary assignments on finitely many observations. The growth function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Π<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℋ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Pi_{\\mathcal H}(m)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the maximum number of such assignments over all sets of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndistinct points. If all\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nassignments are realised, the point set is said to be shattered. The VC\ndimension is the largest number of points for which such a set exists;\nif this number is unbounded, the VC dimension is infinite.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Proof using interval rules<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001729_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Vier realisierbare Beschriftungen von zwei Punkten und eine unmögliche Beschriftung von drei Punkten.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Vier realisierbare Beschriftungen von\nzwei Punkten und eine unmögliche Beschriftung von drei\nPunkten.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: VC-Dimension von Intervallregeln.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>On the real line consider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_{a,b}(x)=\\mathbf{1}_{a\\leq x\\leq b}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\leq b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTwo distinct points can take all four labellings: both positive using an\ninterval containing both, both negative using a distant interval, or\njust one positive using a small interval around that point. The graphic\nshows these four cases. For three ordered points, the assignment\npositive–negative–positive is impossible, because any connected interval\ncontaining both outer points also contains the middle point. Hence the\nVC dimension is exactly 2. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nordered points, every non-empty positive set consists of a contiguous\nblock. There are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m(m+1)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsuch blocks and the empty set. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Pi(m)=1+m(m+1)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Generalisation bounds<\/h2>\n<p>For finite VC dimension\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\ge d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Sauer–Shelah lemma gives the upper bound\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Π<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℋ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>j<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>d<\/mi><\/msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>m<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Pi_{\\mathcal H}(m)\\leq\\sum_{j=0}^{d}\\binom mj<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\ge1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthis sum is at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(em/d)^d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence the number of distinct labellings grows polynomially rather than\nexponentially. A classical VC generalisation statement for binary 0–1\nloss with iid data, under the usual measurability assumptions, is that\nthe uniform discrepancy between empirical and true risk is controlled\nwith probability at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\delta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby a quantity of order<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>O<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>e<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{d\\log(en/d)+\\log(1/\\delta)}{n}}\\right)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge d\\ge1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>O<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnotation hides universal constants; this expression is not a numerically\ncalibrated confidence bound. The proof uses a second independent sample\nfor symmetrisation, counts the possible labellings on the combined\nsample and controls their deviations. Simply substituting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Pi(n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℋ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathcal H|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the finite-class bound would not be justified without this\nargument.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Determine the number of possible interval labellings for five ordered\npoints. Check by enumeration:<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>n <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>labels <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"bu\">tuple<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(n))}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(n):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> b <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(a, n):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        labels.add(<span class=\"bu\">tuple<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>(a<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>j<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>b) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> j <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(n)))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(labels)<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">16<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(labels), <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>n)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Solution: 16 out of 32 possibilities. For the class of one-sided\nthresholds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_a(x)=\\mathbf{1}_{x\\ge a}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe VC dimension is only 1: two ordered points cannot be positive on the\nleft and negative on the right. VC dimension does not simply count\nparameters; it depends on which decisions a class can actually\nrepresent. On its own it measures neither ease of optimisation nor\nrobustness to distribution shifts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://gradml.mit.edu/supervised/learnability_and_vc/\">MIT\n6.790: Learnability and VC Dimension<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Shattering, growth\nfunction<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M17-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M17-L7 – High-dimensional Regression and Feature Selection<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Master: Mathematical Statistics and Statistical Learning (10 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M8&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M8-L5<\/a>,\nM9, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Regularise high-dimensional\nregression, derive soft-thresholding and explain the limitations of\nfeature selection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>More features than observations<\/h2>\n<p>At a test bench, 400 sensor features are calculated from 50\ncomponents. In linear regression\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ϵ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=X\\beta+\\epsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\in\\mathbb R^{50\\times400}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas rank at most 50. Even without measurement noise, different\ncoefficient vectors can yield identical predictions on the sample, since\nthe null space has at least 350 dimensions. Least squares alone does not\nuniquely identify the coefficients. A pseudoinverse selects a particular\nsolution, but this does not imply the physical relevance of all\nfeatures.<\/p>\n<p>Ridge adds a quadratic penalty and Lasso an absolute-value penalty.\nFor centred targets and centred, appropriately scaled columns, a common\nLasso objective is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>β<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">arg<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><\/munder><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mi>β<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat\\beta=\\arg\\min_{\\beta}\\left[\\frac{1}{2n}\\|y-X\\beta\\|_2^2+\\lambda\\|\\beta\\|_1\\right],\\quad\\lambda\\geq0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>An intercept is left unpenalised in typical implementations. Scaling\nis necessary because the penalty acts on coefficient magnitudes. This\nconvention for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust be considered when comparing software.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Soft-thresholding<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001621_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Drei orthogonale Koeffizientenpfade für z=2, −0,8 und 0,3 mit Markierung bei Lambda 0,5.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Drei orthogonale Koeffizientenpfade für\nz=2, −0,8 und 0,3 mit Markierung bei Lambda 0,5.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Lasso-Koeffizientenpfade.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For an orthogonal, normalised design\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X^\\top X/n=I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(thus not in the case\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p&gt;n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),\nthe problem separates into individual coordinates. Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>y<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_j=X_j^\\top y/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUp to constants, each coordinate minimises\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12(b-z_j)^2+\\lambda|b|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndifferentiation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b-z_j+\\lambda=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b-z_j-\\lambda=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe subdifferential of the absolute value includes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[-1,1]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>β<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sign<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat\\beta_j=\\operatorname{sign}(z_j)\\max(|z_j|-\\lambda,0).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=(2,-0.8,0.3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe result is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1.5,-0.3,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe graphic shows how the three paths reach zero as the penalty\nincreases. With the convention\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\|\\beta\\|_2^2/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nRidge would shrink all values by the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(1+\\lambda)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand would not normally set them exactly to zero.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>z <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>, <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">.3<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>lam <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.sign(z)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.maximum(np.<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(z)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>lam, <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(b,[<span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"co\"># KKT: z-b ist im Subgradienten des Strafterms<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.<span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(np.<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(z[b<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>lam)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose((z<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b)[b<span class=\"op\">!=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],lam<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.sign(b[b<span class=\"op\">!=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Selection under correlation<\/h2>\n<p>With general designs, coordinate descent can apply soft-thresholding\nto partial residuals; for non-normalised columns one must additionally\ndivide by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_j^\\top X_j/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf two columns are identical, the Lasso penalty can be the same for\ndifferent same-sign allocations of the same total effect. A selected\nfeature is therefore not evidence of causal relevance. Elastic Net\ncombines L1 and L2 penalties to mitigate problematic correlations.\nClaims about reliable recovery of a sparse true solution require\nadditional assumptions, such as sufficient signal strength and suitable\ndesign conditions.<\/p>\n<p>Choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing inner cross-validation, including scaling and feature selection\nwithin each fold; assess predictive performance on a separate test set\nor in outer cross-validation. Post-selection inference requires special\nmethods. An ordinary confidence interval after data-driven selection may\nbe too narrow.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=(2,-0.8,0.3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermine the solution at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the smallest value for which all coefficients are zero. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1.1,0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{\\max}=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a general design with centred variables the KKT conditions at the\nzero vector give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><msup><mi>X<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_{\\max}=\\|X^\\top y/n\\|_\\infty<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis guarantees the existence of a zero solution, not the truth of a\nnull model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.stat.berkeley.edu/~ryantibs/statlearn-s24/lectures/lasso.pdf\">UC\nBerkeley, Ryan Tibshirani: High-Dimensional Regression: Lasso<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nHigh-dimensional regression, regularisation; PDF and first page opened\nvisually<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M17-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M17-L8 – Robust Estimation and Distribution Shift<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Master: Mathematical Statistics and Statistical Learning (10 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Core/advanced<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M5, M8, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain robust M-estimation and\nweight target risks under covariate shift.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two distinct robustness problems<\/h2>\n<p>A thermometer gives the values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>9<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">9,10,10,11,50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe last value might be a fault, but could also indicate a genuine\noperating state. The mean is 18 and the median is 10. The median\nabsolute deviation from the median (MAD) is 1. For normally distributed\ndata,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.4826<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.4826\\,\\mathrm{MAD}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nserves as an asymptotically calibrated estimate of the standard\ndeviation. A robust method does not mean that rare events are\nunimportant; it primarily reduces the influence of individual extreme\nvalues when estimating a typical location.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Huber loss and bounded slope<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001595_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Quadratischer Score u und Huber-Score mit Schwelle 1,5 über standardisierten Residuen.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Quadratischer Score u und Huber-Score mit\nSchwelle 1,5 über standardisierten Residuen.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Begrenzter Einfluss des Huber-Scores.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For standardised residuals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=(x-\\theta)/s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith fixed scale\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Huber loss is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msup><mi>u<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho_c(u)=\\begin{cases}u^2/2,&amp;|u|\\leq c,\\\\c|u|-c^2/2,&amp;|u|&gt;c.\\end{cases}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Its derivative is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi_c(u)=\\min(c,\\max(-c,u))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe M-estimate minimises\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i\\rho_c((x_i-\\theta)/s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat an interior optimum the sum of the scores is zero. The graphic\ncompares the unbounded quadratic-loss slope with the clipped Huber\nslope. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=1,c=1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe solution for the five measurements is 10.375: the first four\nresiduals have absolute values below 1.5, and the last exceeds the\nthreshold. Thus the score sum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40-4\\theta+1.5=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn regression, high-leverage covariates can still dominate; robust\nresidual treatment alone does not solve this problem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Covariate shift as a distribution problem<\/h2>\n<p>A second plant produces different proportions of the two operating\nmodes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A,B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith unchanged conditional error rates. In training the proportions are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.8,0.2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nin deployment\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.4,0.6)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nassume model error rates of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.05,0.25)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe source risk is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.09<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.09<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the target risk is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.17<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.17<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_T(y\\mid x)=p_S(y\\mid x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand if the target covariate distribution is dominated by the source\ndistribution, rewriting the expectation gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_T(h)=E_S[w(X)\\ell(h(X),Y)],\\qquad w(x)=\\frac{p_T(x)}{p_S(x)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The weights are 0.5 and 3. A new operating state that never occurs in\ntraining cannot be corrected in this way. Highly variable estimated\nweights increase uncertainty; clipping may trade variance for bias. If\nthe conditional distribution changes, this is no longer the pure\ncovariate shift described here.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.optimize <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> brentq<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">9.<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">50<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>theta <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> brentq(<span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> t: np.clip(x<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>t,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.5<\/span>).<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(), <span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">50<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s,t <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>]),np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>e <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.05<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.25<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(theta,<span class=\"fl\">10.375<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(s <span class=\"op\">@<\/span> ((t<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>s)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>e),<span class=\"fl\">.17<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(theta,s<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>e,t<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>e)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>The target plant produces both types equally often. Calculate the\ntarget risk and weights. Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.15<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.05+0.25)/2=0.15<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nweights 0.625 and 2.5. Before applying the method, use labelled target\ndata to check whether the conditional errors really are stable. For the\nlocation estimate, also investigate whether the value 50 is a valid\nmeasurement from another population; a robust estimate should not\nreplace investigation of the underlying cause.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://katsevich-teaching.github.io/stat-9610-notes/robust-estimation.html\">STAT\n9610: Robust estimation and inference<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · MAD, Huber estimation<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v108/li20b.html\">Li, Lam,\nPrusty (2020): Robust Importance Weighting for Covariate Shift<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nCovariate Shift; Abstract and bibliographic details opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M17-L9":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L9\">M17-L9 – Time-to-event Analysis, Censoring and Survival Functions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Master: Mathematical Statistics and Statistical Learning (10 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M4, M6, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Analyse right-censored event\ntimes and distinguish Kaplan–Meier and hazard concepts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Observation time and event time<\/h2>\n<p>Six identical components are tracked from installation. On failure\nthe time is recorded; some components leave the study beforehand for\norganisational reasons. The true event time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis then only bounded below. With censoring time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe observe\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=\\min(T,C)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta=\\mathbf{1}_{T\\leq C}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA right-censored component has neither failed at time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnor been shown to remain failure-free indefinitely. The Kaplan–Meier\nestimator requires suitable independent or non-informative censoring and\na defined population at risk. If particularly failure-prone devices are\nsystematically removed from observation, the estimate may be biased.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Product-limit estimation<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001603_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Überlebenskurve mit Stufen bei Monat 2, 4 und 7, Zensierungen bei Monat 3 und 6.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Überlebenskurve mit Stufen bei Monat 2, 4\nund 7, Zensierungen bei Monat 3 und 6.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Kaplan-Meier-Schätzung mit Zensierung.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The data in months are entirely synthetic:<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Component<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">U<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Event indicator<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>E<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>F<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>We estimate the survival function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(t)=P(T&gt;t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∏<\/mo><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat S(t)=\\prod_{t_j\\leq t}(1-d_j/r_j)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the number of events at time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the risk set size immediately beforehand. Censored observations\nsubsequently leave the risk set but do not directly reduce the estimated\nsurvival probability. When event and censoring times coincide, we use\nthe convention of counting events first.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Month<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">At risk before<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Failures<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Censored<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Estimate afterwards<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5/6<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5/6<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5/12<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">6<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5/12<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The graphic shows the right-continuous step function and censoring\nmarks. The estimated median is the first time with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat S(t)\\leq0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhere month 4. The late fall to zero is based on only one remaining\ncomponent and does not support a certain conclusion about the entire\npopulation.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>u<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> d<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> result<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> time <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> np.unique(u):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    risk<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(u<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span>time).<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    events<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>((u<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>time)<span class=\"op\">&amp;<\/span>(d<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)).<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>()<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    s<span class=\"op\">*=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>events<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>risk<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    result.append(s)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(result,[<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(result)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hazard and modelling<\/h2>\n<p>For continuous event times, the hazard rate is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda(t)=f(t)/S(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprovided\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(t)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt is an instantaneous conditional rate, not a probability; its units\nare 1/month. The cumulative hazard\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∫<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(t)=\\int_0^t\\lambda(u)du<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S(t)=e^{-H(t)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Cox model assumes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda(t\\mid x)=\\lambda_0(t)e^{x^\\top\\beta}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTwo feature vectors then have a constant hazard ratio over time if the\nproportional hazards assumption holds. Alternative models are needed if\nthe ratio changes with time. A log-rank test compares event counts\nbetween groups under suitable assumptions; it does not measure the size\nof an average gain in months.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>If B had failed in the third month instead of being censored, what\nwould the survival estimates be after months 3 and 4? Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5/6\\cdot4/5=2/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nafter two further failures among four components at risk,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/3\\cdot2/4=1/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDistinguishing failure from censoring is therefore crucial. Confidence\nintervals (for example Greenwood-based transformations) require special\ntreatment when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>S<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat S=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor 1; small risk sets should be displayed explicitly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://stats.oarc.ucla.edu/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/survival_r_full.html\">UCLA\nOARC: Introduction to Survival Analysis in R<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Censoring,\nKaplan–Meier<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M17-L10":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L10\">M17-L10 – Multivariate Statistics and Joint Modelling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M17 – Master: Mathematical Statistics and Statistical Learning (10 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Elective specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, M8, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M17&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M17-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Use covariance in joint models\nand calculate a Hotelling T² test for a mean vector.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Joint rather than separate outcomes<\/h2>\n<p>At a plant, two dimensionless, already scaled measurement deviations\nare recorded for each component. Both may be affected by the same\nmechanical cause. Separate tests ignore the joint dispersion structure\nand, when making multiple decisions, the accumulation of errors.\nMultivariate statistics models the random vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">ℝ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\in\\mathbb R^p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing the mean vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=E[X]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand covariance matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Sigma=E[(X-\\mu)(X-\\mu)^\\top]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>With a positive definite covariance matrix, the multivariate normal\ndensity is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x)=(2\\pi)^{-p/2}|\\Sigma|^{-1/2}\\exp[-\\tfrac12(x-\\mu)^\\top\\Sigma^{-1}(x-\\mu)].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The quadratic form is the squared Mahalanobis distance. A joint\ndeviation in a direction of high variance counts less than an equally\nlarge deviation in a stable direction. The covariance matrix therefore\ndetermines not only marginal dispersion but also directions in feature\nspace. PCA uses its eigenvectors for dimensionality reduction but does\nnot itself test a mean.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hotelling T² and joint uncertainty<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001594_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Elliptische Annahmeregion bei positiv korrelierten Messwerten; Vektoren gleicher Länge in Haupt- und Nebenrichtung.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Elliptische Annahmeregion bei positiv\nkorrelierten Messwerten; Vektoren gleicher Länge in Haupt- und\nNebenrichtung.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Gemeinsame Mittelwertregion.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For an iid sample from a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-dimensional\nnormal distribution with unknown positive definite covariance and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n&gt;p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlet\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the sample covariance with denominator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>:<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0:\\mu=\\mu_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>∼<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">under <\/mtext><mspace width=\"0.333em\"><\/mspace><\/mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2=n(\\bar x-\\mu_0)^\\top S^{-1}(\\bar x-\\mu_0),\\qquad \\frac{n-p}{p(n-1)}T^2\\sim F_{p,n-p}\\quad\\text{under }H_0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With two features and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsuppose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=(0.4,0.4)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>⊤<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_0=(0,0)^\\top<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0.8\\\\0.8&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe eigenvalue in direction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis 1.8, in the contrasting direction 0.2. For the observed deviation,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.32<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1.8<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.556<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2=20\\cdot0.32/1.8\\approx3.556<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe F statistic is approximately 1.684 and the p-value approximately\n0.214. For an alternative observed deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.4,-0.4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe would have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>32<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2=32<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the same covariance. Both vectors have equal length, but the second\ndirection is more remarkable. The graphic shows the joint 95% acceptance\nregion for the observed mean at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a specified null value.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.stats <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> f<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>n,p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>S<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> v <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>]),np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>])]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    T2<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>n<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>v<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>np.linalg.solve(S,v)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    F<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(n<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>T2<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(p<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(n<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(T2,F,f.sf(F,p,n<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(n<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">@<\/span>np.linalg.solve(S,[<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>]),<span class=\"dv\">32<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Joint modelling and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>A multivariate regression model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y=XB+E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith several target columns can model correlated residuals through a\nshared covariance. If each column uses the same regressors and error\nvectors are uncorrelated across observations with constant positive\ndefinite covariance, the ordinary least-squares coefficients for each\ncolumn coincide with the GLS result from joint modelling; the joint\ninference may nevertheless differ. With many outcomes relative to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes singular or numerically unstable. Covariance shrinkage or\ndimensionality reduction may be useful, but a modified statistic\nrequires its own calibration. A multivariate normal distribution cannot\nbe inferred merely from apparently normal marginal distributions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=I_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the example above. What statistic do both deviation vectors give?\nSolution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.16<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.16<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2=20(0.16+0.16)=6.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThey are equally unusual under isotropic covariance; the F statistic is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>18<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>6.4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>38<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.032<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">18\\cdot6.4/38\\approx3.032<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nHotelling’s\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>T<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreduces to the square of the usual one-sample t statistic. Joint\nrejection of a null hypothesis does not automatically identify the\nindividual feature responsible; subsequent tests must be controlled\naccordingly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section5/pmc543.htm\">NIST/SEMATECH\ne-Handbook: Hotelling’s T squared<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Joint mean test<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
